<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Headless.XUnit" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.13.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The App reference is the suite's subject; the Mcp one is there for a single seam.
    `render_frame` spans both projects — the app renders the base64, the MCP tool wraps it
    for the client — and the half-tested version of that (IpcTests, app side only) is exactly
    what let B192 ship: every assertion passed on a payload nobody then checked the wrapping
    of. McpImageBlockTests drives both halves, which needs both projects in one suite.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Lightbox.App\Lightbox.App.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Lightbox.Mcp\Lightbox.Mcp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    One implementation of the contact-sheet writer, shared rather than copied. Linked instead of
    packaged because it is test tooling: putting it in a src project would ship a PNG debug helper
    inside the application, and copying it would let the two suites' sheets drift apart in exactly
    the way that makes two pictures impossible to compare.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Lightbox.Raster.Tests\VisualSheet.cs" Link="VisualSheet.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The PSD fixture builder, linked for the same reason: the reader is tested in the Raster suite
    and the mapping onto a document is tested here, and both need to hand the same bytes in. Two
    copies of a hand-written binary format builder would drift, and the whole point of the fixtures
    is that a failure says which field disagreed rather than "the bytes changed".
  -->
  <!--
    Recorded input traces, copied beside the assembly so InputTraceReplayTests can read
    them at run time. They are read as files rather than embedded because the point is that
    the file the reporter sends is the file the harness loads: an embedded copy would let the
    on-disk format drift from the one a capture actually arrives in, which is the one thing
    this fixture set exists to prevent.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="Fixtures\**" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Lightbox.Raster.Tests\PsdFixture.cs" Link="PsdFixture.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\Lightbox.Raster.Tests\PsdLayerFixture.cs" Link="PsdLayerFixture.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
